.Dd November 26, 2026
.Dt PI0-PGP 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pi0-pgp
.Nd make OpenPGP keys offline and move them to a YubiKey
.Sh DESCRIPTION
To make an OpenPGP key:
.Bl -enum
.It
Plug the YubiKey and a FAT USB stick labelled KEYS.
.It
Set the clock, which starts each boot at the time the image was made:
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
timedatectl set-time 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS UTC'
.Ed
.It
Run the key generator,
.Pa /usr/bin/gpg-genkey :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
PASSPHRASE='...' gpg-genkey -n 'Your Name' -e you@example.org
.Ed
.El
.Pp
The header comment of the script lists its options.
.Ss Memory and USB sticks
The secret key is made in memory, under
.Pa ~/.gnupg ,
which never swaps, and leaves only on the stick, which systemd mounts on
.Pa /mnt/keys
when something reads it and unmounts after 10 seconds of disuse: wait that
long before pulling it out.
Any other FAT stick is mounted the same way on
.Pa /mnt/usb-storage .
The SD card is read-only: whatever is written elsewhere is in memory, and gone
when the Pi powers off.
.Ss Finding a USB device
USB sticks and drives are sda, sdb and so on, in the order the Pi finds them,
and mmcblk0 is the SD card, which is root's.
Their models and sizes, and the file systems they hold, tell them apart:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fdisk -l
blkid
.Ed
.Pp
The regular account writes to them: check the name before any command here that
erases one.
.Ss Partitioning
.Ic cfdisk ,
which has a menu,
.Ic fdisk
and
.Ic sfdisk
write partition tables.
This erases /dev/sda, and gives it a partition for each of FAT, ext4 and BTRFS,
the last taking the rest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sfdisk --wipe always --wipe-partitions always /dev/sda <<END
label: gpt
size=1GiB, type=EBD0A0A2-B9E5-4433-87C0-68B6B72699C7
size=1GiB, type=L
type=L
END
.Ed
.Pp
sfdisk and fdisk tell the regular account that re-reading the partition table
failed: udev has the kernel read it as the device is closed.
.Ss Making and checking file systems
These erase what the partition holds, and label the new file system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkfs.fat -F 32 -n PHOTOS /dev/sda1
mkfs.ext4 -L archive /dev/sda2
mkfs.btrfs -L backup /dev/sda3
.Ed
.Pp
Where there is a file system, mkfs.ext4 asks before it erases it, and
mkfs.btrfs wants
.Fl f .
mkfs.btrfs warns the regular account that it could not register the device,
which udev does.
These check them while they are not mounted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fsck.fat -n /dev/sda1
fsck.ext4 -f /dev/sda2
btrfs check /dev/sda3
.Ed
.Pp
fsck.ext4 asks before it repairs what it finds; the other two change nothing.
BTRFS has no manual pages: see
.Ic btrfs --help
and
.Ic mkfs.btrfs --help .
.Ss Moving files
The regular account reads and writes the FAT file systems that systemd mounts.
root mounts ext4 and BTRFS, and any further device, and unmounts them before
the device is pulled out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount --mkdir -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sdb2 /tmp/archive
cp -r /mnt/usb-storage/photos /tmp/archive
umount /tmp/archive
.Ed
.Pp
Mounting a file system has the kernel read whatever it holds: after a device
that you do not trust, power off before you work on secrets.
.Ss Copying devices
.Ic dd
copies a device or a partition to another, or to a file, block by block.
This copies /dev/sda over /dev/sdb, which must be at least as large:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/sdb bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress
.Ed
.Pp
dd overwrites what
.Cm of=
names without asking, mounted or not.
Writing over flash storage does not erase it for certain, as it keeps blocks
that no write reaches.
.Ss Formatting a stick for the keys
To format a stick for the keys, find it with
.Ic blkid :
it is sda, and mmcblk0 is the SD card.
This erases the stick:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkfs.fat -I -F 32 -n KEYS /dev/sda
.Ed
.Ss Moving the subkeys to the YubiKey
To move the subkeys to the YubiKey, use
.Ic gpg\ --edit-key
and
.Ic keytocard :
the keys stay in memory until the Pi powers off.
These show what the keys and the YubiKey hold:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpg --list-secret-keys
gpg --card-status
ykman info
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dd 1 ,
.Xr gpg 1 ,
.Xr blkid 8 ,
.Xr cfdisk 8 ,
.Xr e2fsck 8 ,
.Xr fdisk 8 ,
.Xr fsck.fat 8 ,
.Xr mke2fs 8 ,
.Xr mkfs.fat 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr sfdisk 8
